        <groundspeak:log id="619423299">
          <groundspeak:date>2016-07-12T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="946600">Black Shark</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP656 Poplar Park&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Egads!  &lt;img src="/images/icons/icon_smile_shock.gif" border="0" align="middle" /&gt;  There was a solid line of vehicles parked on the street beside the park and the park itself was swarming with muggles sized XL to XXXS.  &quot;Parent Link Centre&quot; the sandwich board sign said and a &quot;Play Engineer&quot; (as identified by her t-shirt) was seated at a nearby table.  &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We were surprised to find an open spot to park quite close to GZ and the GPS arrow suggested the cap might be a comfortable distance from the crowd. We decided to get out and check further and found that it was a very suitable distance away in our opinion...and it seemed noone else was concerned, either.  After all, how could anyone be suspicious of two businesslike people equipped with a camera, trowel, GPS and clipboard?  Not even departing children walking by questioned our actions.  We would have happily showed them the shiny piece of brass we were uncovering. Digging for treasure, we were, arrr!  Surely the Play Engineer would have approved!  Rain had washed a layer of dirt over the cap and one side of the hole needed enlarging to fully expose the circumference.  The cap is in great condition with all letters and numbers easily readable.  We aimed our camera thoughtfully for context photos and were soon gone.  Thanks for another cap to start another day!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
